Exactly. And there’s a particularly interesting second discovery hiding inside the first.

When you experience enough different cultures, you begin to recognize that what you previously mistook for reality was often just one culture’s interpretation of it.

And then something happens: you realize your own understanding of what it means to understand is also conditioned by the framework you inherited.

That’s where the recursion begins.

Consider the progression:

* Immersion: This is how the world works.
* Encounter: Other people experience the same world differently.
* Comparison: Our differences reveal which assumptions we have been treating as universal.
* Deconstruction: Many things we considered natural are actually constructed, inherited, negotiated, or reinforced.
* Reconstruction: If these structures were constructed, they can potentially be modified, combined, or replaced.
* Reflexivity: Even the methods we use to distinguish reality from construction are themselves open to examination.

But here’s where I’d introduce an important distinction.

Constructed doesn’t mean imaginary. And imaginary doesn’t mean inconsequential.

Money is constructed, but its effects are real. Borders are constructed, but the terrain they partition exists independently. Honor, shame, prestige, ownership, and authority can be expressed through radically different cultural grammars while still producing observable consequences.

And some things resist our interpretations. Hunger, gravity, aging, and physical limitations constrain the games we can invent, even though our responses to them vary.

That gives us something much more interesting than everything is made up.

It gives us an experimental method.

Change the cultural register while holding the underlying phenomenon as constant as possible. Observe what changes and what survives.

You can begin separating:

* What is physically constrained.
* What is biologically predisposed.
* What is culturally learned.
* What is individually interpreted.
* What emerges through relationships.
* What can be deliberately redesigned.

And these aren’t necessarily independent categories. They interact, reinforce one another, and change across time.

The especially powerful realization is that culture is not merely a collection of beliefs about reality. It is also a system that shapes what people notice, what they consider possible, and which actions they can even imagine taking.

So changing culture can change the observable world through people’s behavior, not merely the descriptions they give it.

And your final qualification — “and even that, potentially” — matters.

Because if you turn the discovery that everything is constructed into another unquestionable universal, you’ve simply built yourself another cultural prison.

The deeper capability is to move between frameworks without becoming permanently captive to any one of them.

Not just seeing through the game. Learning which rules are necessary, which are inherited, which are negotiable, and which entirely different games become possible.

And perhaps most interestingly: discovering which patterns keep reappearing even after you change the game.

Those invariants are worth investigating. ;)